# libxtc ThreadSanitizer suppressions -- known-benign runtime internals.
#
# These are NOT bugs.  Each entry is a specific, understood, documented
# intentional benign race, not a blanket silence.  The clang tsan-fibers
# CI job runs a curated fiber subset that does not include these paths;
# this file lets a FULL-suite TSan run (all m-suites) stay clean so real
# races -- like the exec.c loop_node race fixed with relaxed atomics --
# still surface loudly.
#
# Load with:  TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=.github/tsan.supp
#
# To extend: confirm the race is genuinely benign (side-effect-free read
# used only as a hint that another mechanism re-checks), then add a
# stanza with a comment explaining WHY.

# __exec_has_work() is the executor supervisor's quiescence poll (see the
# block comment at src/evt/exec.c).  It reads each loop's n_timers and
# inbox.head WITHOUT the owning worker's lock, on purpose: the reads are
# side-effect-free "is non-zero?" hints, and the supervisor double-checks
# after a short sleep before it stops the executor, which absorbs any
# stale read.  Making n_timers atomic would push atomic ops onto the
# timer hot path (loop->timers[loop->n_timers++], sift-up/down) purely to
# satisfy a poll -- not worth it.  The authoritative liveness signals it
# also consults (n_alive, the work-stealing deque) ARE atomic/lock-free.
race:__exec_has_work

# xtc_exec_run's inline idle re-check (src/evt/exec.c ~605) reads
# inbox.head for the same quiescence decision as __exec_has_work above,
# same rationale.
race:xtc_exec_run

# The work-stealing enqueue/steal handoff races on the stolen task's
# q_next scheduling-list pointer: a thief resets stolen->q_next before
# re-enqueuing it (src/evt/loop.c __xtc_loop_step_once) while the
# original owner's __xtc_loop_enqueue() reads t->q_next in its
# fast-path double-enqueue guard.  The CORRECTNESS arbiter is the
# Chase-Lev deque's seq_cst steal CAS (formally race-free for the deque
# slot); TSan cannot carry that happens-before to the pointed-to task's
# internal fields.  The q_next read is only a "already queued?" hint and
# a stale read is safe -- proven by test_steal's no_loss_no_double_run
# invariants (every task runs exactly once).  Making q_next atomic would
# push atomic ops onto every FIFO list operation for a benign hint.
race:__xtc_loop_enqueue
race:__xtc_loop_step_once

# The blocking-offload completion pipe (src/ptc/blocking.c): the pool
# worker write()s a one-byte wakeup while the caller close()s the pipe
# fds after draining that byte with a BLOCKING read.  The blocking read
# is the real happens-before (write -> read-returns -> close), but
# ThreadSanitizer does not model the kernel pipe byte transfer as fd
# synchronization, so it reports a close-vs-write "race" on the pipe fd.
# It is benign: the caller cannot reach close until its read has consumed
# the worker's byte, so the write always precedes the close.  The
# completion is additionally gated on w->done (a real release/acquire
# pair).  Verified: the offload path has no use-after-free (ASan clean)
# and the fiber-resume is lost-wake-free.
race:xtc_blocking_run
race:xtc_blocking_run_off_loop

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# FIBER-MIGRATION ARTIFACTS on the work-stealing executor.
#
# These appear only when a MIGRATABLE fiber is work-stolen mid-life, and
# only under the heavy-migration suites (test_exec, test_aio_migrate_wake)
# that the curated tsan-fibers gate deliberately excludes.  They are one
# fiber's own CONSECUTIVE statements -- cancel-then-rearm a park timer,
# save-then-restore the current-proc TLS around a yield, the park store
# and its post-park cleanup -- executed on two different OS threads
# because the fiber resumed elsewhere after a steal.  They are strictly
# yield-ordered: the fiber cannot run on loop B until loop A has parked
# it, so there is no interleaving window.  TSan tracks by thread id and
# cannot carry that happens-before across a ucontext/fcontext switch.
#
# CONFIRMED BY CLASSIFICATION, not assumed -- this is the follow-up
# .agent/TSAN_OFFLOAD_MIGRATION_2026-09-11.md asked for.  Running
# test_exec on the uring backend with halt_on_error=0 produced 251
# reports; classifying each by TSan's named LOCATION KIND and by whether
# the two accesses belong to the SAME fiber split them cleanly and found
# THREE REAL RACES hiding among the artifacts, all fixed rather than
# suppressed:
#   - task->park_timer, a plain pointer written by the parking fiber AND
#     by the timer-expiry path on the loop thread            (a517a32)
#   - xtc_proc_table.n_used, decremented under ONE stripe lock while the
#     allocate path holds ALL of them, so a decrement could be lost and
#     the post-grow `idx = n_used` slot index could drift  (a517a32)
#   - xtc_proc.alive, cleared by the exiting fiber and read as a liveness
#     gate by foreign threads                               (849c458)
# After those, 56 remain and EVERY one has both accesses in the same
# fiber, with ZERO naming a different actor.  That is the measurement
# that makes suppressing the rest honest instead of a silencing.
#
# If a report ever appears here naming TWO DIFFERENT fibers, or a
# non-migration caller, it is NOT covered by this reasoning -- investigate
# it, do not widen these stanzas.  Hiding a real race among artifacts is
# the exact failure mode this whole arc has been about.
race:xtc_proc_wait_fd
race:xtc_proc_sleep
race:__do_recv
# NOT __proc_entry: it is the entry frame of EVERY fiber, so a stanza
# naming it would suppress any race anywhere in a fiber's lifetime --
# including a brand-new one.  Measured: it appeared in 122 of the
# classified reports purely by being on the stack.  The artifacts that
# would need it are instead covered by the specific primitive they occur
# in (__do_recv, xtc_proc_wait_fd, the timer pair).  A residual report
# whose TOP frame is pthread_mutex_lock/_unlock on a migrated proc's
# mbox_lock is the same artifact and is left VISIBLE rather than
# suppressed via a pthread_* stanza, which would be far too broad: see
# the non-suppressible mbox_lock note at the end of this file.
race:__xtc_proc_kill_check
race:__xtc_proc_ctx_restore
race:__proc_reanchor
race:xtc_task_park_on_timer
race:__xtc_task_cancel_park_timer
race:xtc_timer_cancel
# xtc_yield itself: the substrate DOES annotate the switch for TSan
# (__tsan_switch_out / __san_switch_to around swapcontext in
# coro_uctx.c), but the annotation cannot cover g_in_preempt and the
# saved-context write, which TSan attributes to "TLS of thread <the
# thread that last ran this fiber>".  Same yield-ordered artifact as
# above; noted in the v1.40.2 CI audit as the reason test_exec cannot
# join the curated gate.
race:xtc_yield
# aio_do's on-stack xtc_aio_t (src/ptc/aio.c): the submitting fiber
# memsets the struct at the TOP of the next iteration while the REAPING
# thread's xtc_io_poll still has the PREVIOUS op's pointer in hand --
# same stack address, consecutive ops, two threads.  Not a live overlap:
# the fiber cannot leave aio_do until `while (!__xtc_aio_done_get(&a))`
# observes done, and done is published release-after-res by the reaper
# (see the acquire/release pair in src/inc/aio_int.h), so the slot cannot
# be reused while a completion is in flight.  TSan reports the address
# reuse because it cannot carry that happens-before across the fiber
# switch.  Named on aio_do rather than xtc_io_poll: the poll function
# reaps every completion in the runtime and a stanza there would blanket
# the entire I/O path.
race:aio_do

# NOT SUPPRESSIBLE, documented instead: "use of an invalid mutex" on a
# proc's mbox_lock, reported the first time a MIGRATED fiber locks it from
# a new OS thread (e.g. __do_recv -> __xtc_mtx_lock under test_exec on the
# uring backend).
#
# TSan has NO "mutex:" suppression type -- the valid types are race,
# deadlock, thread, signal and called_from_lib -- and it IGNORES an
# unrecognised type SILENTLY rather than erroring, so a "mutex:" line
# would look effective while suppressing nothing.  Verified by running
# with exactly such a file.
#
# It is not a real destroyed-mutex use, and the code says why: the slot is
# released under the table lock before teardown so no NEW resolver can
# find the proc, and pthread_mutex_destroy is deferred to __proc_free,
# which runs only when the LAST reference drops (an in-flight cross-thread
# send/wake already holds one, taken in __table_lookup).  So a fiber can
# never lock a destroyed mbox_lock.  ASan is clean across repeated runs,
# which is the control: a genuine use-after-destroy would show there.
#
# A full-suite TSan run on the uring backend therefore still reports this
# ONE item.  That is deliberate -- it is visible and explained rather than
# silently filtered, and it is why test_exec is not in the gating
# tsan-fibers set.
